<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#son-of-heaven">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Son of Heaven</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tianzi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Son of Heaven (tianzi) was a title of the Chinese ruler, first used by the kings of the Western Zhou, expressing the idea that the ruler held a mandate from Heaven to govern all under Heaven. Emperors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> used it until the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> in 1912, and it was also adopted by rulers in neighbouring lands such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/son-of-heaven.jpg" alt="Son of Heaven" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nie Chongyi 聂崇义 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%8E%89%E8%BC%85.%E6%96%B0%E5%AE%9A%E4%B8%89%E7%A4%BC%E5%9B%BE.20%E5%8D%B7.%E5%AE%8B%E8%81%82%E5%B4%87%E4%B9%89%E9%9B%86%E6%B3%A8.%E6%B8%85%E5%BA%B7%E7%86%9912%E5%B9%B4%E9%80%9A%E5%BF%97%E5%A0%82%E5%88%8A.1673%E5%B9%B4.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
